Finding 1: Parameter Configuration Functions as Learner-Intuitive Scaffolding

Across the controlled experiment and two naturalistic classroom phases, parameter settings reliably shaped interaction type distributions and were experienced by learners as distinct scaffolding modes. In Phase 1, the structured condition (T=0.3, top-p=0.4) produced a tutor-like profile dominated by Type A constraint repair (60%) and Type B exemplar giving (35%), while Type C surprise harvest appeared only 5%. In the exploratory condition (T=0.8, top-p=0.9), Type C rose to 35%, a 7-fold increase (chi-squared = 24.3, p < .001, Cramer's V = .38), indicating a robust shift toward divergent, low-directive collaboration (Figure 1).

When participants could choose freely, their behavior echoed these distributions. In Phase 2, 75% voluntarily selected the exploratory room; in Phase 3, 66.7% chose exploratory, 8.3% chose structured, and 62% switched between modes within a single session. These convergent patterns suggest that parameter configuration is not merely a technical setting but a phenomenologically real scaffold that learners can detect, interpret, and strategically deploy through felt interactional texture. As participants described, exploratory AI offered "random non-related stuff" that sparked new thinking (P12), while structured AI felt "like a menu" of options (P14). Together, the evidence supports the claim that parameter manipulation functions as an intuitive pedagogical lever that regulates which interaction types become available and how authorship and agency are experienced.
